  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p><strong>Mount Spickard</strong> rises to 8,970 feet in Washington. With roughly 4,800 feet of topographic prominence, it stands well above the surrounding terrain. The Mount Spickard Trail covers 8.1 miles round trip (out-and-back) with about 2,838 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a steady climb. It's rated Strenuous overall.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>Most of the climbing comes early \u2014 the first half of the route does the bulk of the work before the grade eases. The steepest stretch arrives around mile 2.31, gaining roughly 3,200 feet per mile \u2014 expect a slow, deliberate pace through here. From the trailhead at about 2,900 feet, the route tops out near 5,686 feet. Along the way the route passes a waterfall (Depot Creek Falls) near mile 2.66 \u2014 useful checkpoints for judging your pace.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=48.99683,-121.32444\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">48.99683, -121.32444</a> (tap for driving directions). It's roughly 105 miles northeast of Seattle, Washington. Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Late May through October is the main season. Snow and ice linger into spring and return early in fall at this latitude and elevation; in winter this becomes a different, more serious undertaking requiring traction devices and warm layers.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Strenuous rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 4.0 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Mount Prophet, Jack Mountain and Mount Shuksan \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
